WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760
Speaker 1: Well, the Republican Party is finally&nbsp;
behaving rationally. Republicans are panicking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:12.120
that the now multiple arrests of failed former&nbsp;
president twice impeached, twice indicted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:19.620
Donald Trump may actually boomerang on them and&nbsp;
be damaging. Big picture. There's an interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:27.060
article in the Hill by Alexander Bolton called&nbsp;
Republicans fear Trump Legal Woes will boomerang&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:32.340
on them. Senate Republicans are worried that&nbsp;
Trump's legal troubles will create a major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:38.220
headwind for Republican candidates in 2024. They&nbsp;
say the battle between the Justice Department and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:44.520
Trump, who pleaded not guilty Tuesday just as his&nbsp;
unsubstantiated claims that the 2020 election was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:50.400
stolen, became a prominent point of debate in last&nbsp;
year's primaries. They worry Trump's dominance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:55.500
the media spotlight will turn off swing voters,&nbsp;
especially suburban women, and hurt their chances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:00.840
of taking back the Senate or protecting their&nbsp;
small House majority. The article goes on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:06.600
include a number of quotes from different&nbsp;
Republicans. This is a logical reaction to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:12.180
what is going on now. There is a group of people&nbsp;
that is choosing to narrowly focus only on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:16.260
presidential race, and we will look at the polling&nbsp;
in the presidential race a little bit later today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:22.140
You could convince yourself and maybe others,&nbsp;
if you look only at the presidential race,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:29.520
that it is quite binary and it doesn't have any&nbsp;
trickle down effect. What is happening with Donald&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:34.680
Trump? Let me tell you what I mean by that. You&nbsp;
could look at the Trump arrests and look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:42.660
Republican primary and say to yourself, listen, if&nbsp;
Trump's arrests hurt Trump, someone else will win&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:48.420
the primary and then that person will face Joe&nbsp;
Biden. And they might win or they might lose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:53.520
and it will have nothing to do with what is going&nbsp;
on with Trump. In other words, the context is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.520 --> 00:02:01.140
Trump's legal troubles will only affect who is the&nbsp;
Republican nominee. And beyond that, it will not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:07.620
have any broader impact on the Republican Party.&nbsp;
There are two problems with that relatively narrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:13.920
focus. Problem number one is that indeed,&nbsp;
if Trump is leading the Republican primary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:19.500
for several more months, but then he's arrested a&nbsp;
third time and potentially arrested a fourth time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:24.720
and then it becomes clear he can't really campaign&nbsp;
because he's just going from courthouse to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:31.980
courthouse and Ron DeSantis or someone else ends&nbsp;
up winning the primary. The impact of that last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:37.020
minute collapse by Donald Trump doesn't end with&nbsp;
the Republican primary. And there is the belief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:41.760
and I believe it is a warranted belief,&nbsp;
that if such a shakeup were to take place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:47.940
it would weaken whoever is the eventual Republican&nbsp;
nominee against Joe Biden. So that's one way in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:53.460
which thinking of the Trump arrests only is&nbsp;
relevant to the Republican primary is wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.460 --> 00:03:00.060
But what Republicans are increasingly concerned&nbsp;
about now is very serious, which is that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:07.440
entire circus atmosphere and sideshow nature&nbsp;
of what is going on, which also, by the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:12.180
includes some Republican candidates saying I&nbsp;
would pardon Trump and others saying I wouldn't,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:17.760
and this just becoming the circus that it is.&nbsp;
It may just be very bad for random Republican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:22.740
senators seeking reelection. Four random members&nbsp;
of the House of Representatives, some who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:28.500
supporters of Trump and some who are not, that it&nbsp;
will create a broader problem for any Republican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:35.220
that is running in November of 24, including&nbsp;
simply that the Trump fiasco might reduce turnout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:40.860
and make some voters stay home who would otherwise&nbsp;
vote to reelect some of these Republicans. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:48.420
too early to know whether that fear is backed by&nbsp;
data, but intuitively, it makes a ton of sense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:53.940
We'll leave that question there. And now I want&nbsp;
to look a little bit at some new national polling.

